<main class="main-content article-main" id="main-content"><div class="article-shell about-shell">
  <div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span>About the atlas</span></div>
  <header class="page-masthead reveal"><div class="masthead-copy"><span class="article-eyebrow"><span class="kicker-line"></span> ABOUT / WHY THIS ATLAS EXISTS</span><h1>More than<br><em>mountain scenery.</em></h1><p class="article-deck">A generous, visual learning space for exploring mountains as connected systems — geology and ecology, water and weather, wildlife and human communities.</p></div><div class="masthead-art"><img src="<%= imageSrc('about.svg') %>" data-image-fallback="/img/about.svg" alt="A layered mountain landscape"><span>AN OPEN LEARNING ATLAS</span></div></header>
  <section class="about-story reveal"><div class="about-quote"><span>THE IDEA</span><blockquote>Follow the connections, and the landscape starts to make sense.</blockquote></div><div class="prose"><h2>Knowledge arranged like a landscape</h2><p>This site is organised as a multi-page atlas rather than a single long article. Start with the physical processes that make mountains, visit regional case studies, then explore the living communities and people whose lives depend on these high places.</p><p>Mountain systems are complex, and no single summary can represent every region or community. The articles provide entry points and broad concepts; reference links point toward scientific institutions and further reading for details that deserve closer scrutiny.</p><h2>How to explore</h2><p>Use the collapsible contents rail for a full map of the site, dropdowns for quick navigation, search to find a subject across pages, and the interactive elevation lab to think about habitat change. The glossary gives concise definitions, while the knowledge check helps reveal which ideas are worth revisiting.</p><h2>A note on illustrations</h2><p>The bundled artwork is locally stored SVG illustration, designed to keep the project self-contained and easy to run without external image services. Replace those files with your own photographs in <code>public/img</code> at any time. See the image guide for suggestions and attribution reminders.</p><h2>Learning with care</h2><p>Mountain environments support distinct cultures and vulnerable ecosystems. Treat local communities as knowledge holders, verify regional claims and species ranges, follow official outdoor safety guidance, and observe wildlife from a respectful distance.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="/mountain-earth">Start your journey <span>↗</span></a></div></section>
</div><%- include('../partials/footer') %></main>
<%- include('../partials/layout-end') %>
